From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emmanuel Velez Garcia <velezgarciaemmanuel25@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 08:47:11 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Internal profiler and /vs perf

Spigot's timings answer "which plugin", which is the wrong question on a
server whose cost is chunk I/O and world count. This adds counters for
what a prison/PvP fork actually spends its tick on, read back with
/vs perf:

  /vs perf            TPS, MSPT percentiles, main thread breakdown,
                      chunk totals, network, GC and heap
  /vs perf chunks     per world: sync/async loads, generations, unloads,
                      retains, chunks and bytes sent
  /vs perf worlds     worlds ranked by main thread time
  /vs perf packets    packet and byte rates, and how much of the outgoing
                      traffic is chunk data
  /vs perf entities   entity, tile entity and player counts per world
  /vs perf reset      clear the counters

Counters are LongAdders incremented on paths that are already reading a
file or building a 90 KB packet, so they are always on. The per-section
System.nanoTime() timers sit behind debug.profiler, and cost about ten
clock reads per world per tick. Time spent inside a blocking chunk load
is measured unconditionally, because that is the number needed to find
which world and which plugin path is stalling the tick, and a load that
hits the disk already dwarfs the measurement.

Chunk.getChunkMapSize() computes a chunk packet's payload size from its
non-empty section count without building the packet, so byte accounting
does not pay the cost it exists to measure.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

